// BASELINE: WALE LARGE-EDDY CLOSURE
// The jet and edge region can generate unsteady shear and vortices. LES evolves the
// resolved motion and models smaller-scale momentum transport. WALE uses local
// velocity-gradient information and has useful near-wall asymptotic behaviour.
// cubeRootVol defines its filter length from cell volume; stretched cells can have
// very different directional resolution despite the same filter width.
// 
// Alternatives: laminar means no subgrid model, NOT a promise of DNS resolution.
// Smagorinsky is another LES closure; dynamic closures adapt coefficients at added
// complexity/cost. RANS/URANS models much more of the turbulence and can change the
// unsteady jet/acoustic feedback. None is universally correct for every ocarina.
// We fix WALE for baseline comparisons, then vary mesh/time resolution before claiming
// an audible difference is caused by geometry. No wall functions or prism layers are
// used in this baseline; wall/shear/thermal losses still require a resolution study.

// WALE large-eddy simulation models unresolved momentum transport from local gradients.
// The cubeRootVol filter width follows cell volume. Choosing LES does not by itself
// resolve turbulence: spatial/time resolution and near-wall treatment still need study.

FoamFile
{
    version     2.0;
    format      ascii;
    class       dictionary;
    object      turbulenceProperties;
}

simulationType  LES;

LES
{
    LESModel        WALE;
    turbulence      on;
    printCoeffs     yes;
    delta           cubeRootVol;
}

